@endnode

@node g-what-you-need "What you need"
@next g-the-first-program

@{b}What you need@{ub}

  - AmigaOS 2.04 or newer, any 680x0.
  - LIBS:fuzzy.library: the build for the processor (build/000, 020 or
    060, see the README). No floating point, so no FPU build.
  - The headers in include/: libraries/fuzzy.h, proto/fuzzy.h,
    inline/fuzzy.h (gcc) and clib/fuzzy_protos.h. fuzzy.sfd has the calls
    and registers for other languages.
  - For the index: one made by FuzzyFind's updatedb (or by your own
    program, below).

@endnode

@node g-the-first-program "The first program"
@next g-matching
@prev g-what-you-need

@{b}The first program@{ub}

The five best matches for a word, from the index:

    #include <stdio.h>
    #include <proto/exec.h>
    #include <proto/fuzzy.h>

    struct Library *FuzzyBase;

    int main(int argc, char **argv)
    {
        struct FuzzyIndex *index;
        struct FuzzyResults *r;
        LONG err, i;

        if (argc < 2) {
            printf("usage: best WORD\\n");
            return 10;
        }
        if (!(FuzzyBase = OpenLibrary(FUZZYNAME, 0))) {
            printf("needs fuzzy.library\\n");
            return 20;
        }
        if (!(index = FuzzyOpenIndex(NULL, &err))) {        /* NULL: the usual place */
            printf("no index: %s\\n", FuzzyErrorText(err));
            CloseLibrary(FuzzyBase);
            return 10;
        }
        if ((r = FuzzySearch(index, argv[1], 0, 5))) {     /* the 5 best */
            printf("%ld of %ld entries match\\n", (long)r->Matched, (long)index->Count);
            for (i = 0; i < r->Count; i++)
                printf("%5ld  %s\\n", (long)r->Hits[i].Score, r->Hits[i].Path);
            FuzzyFreeResults(r);
        }
        FuzzyCloseIndex(index);
        CloseLibrary(FuzzyBase);
        return 0;
    }

best multiview on a Workbench 3.2.1 disk prints (run 2026-10-17):

    3 of 1575 entries match
      250  Workbench:Utilities/MultiView
      250  Workbench:Utilities/MultiView.info
      250  Workbench:Locale/Help/english/Sys/Utilities/multiview.help

Equal scores go shortest path first.

Build with bebbo's gcc: m68k-amigaos-gcc -Os -noixemul -Wno-pointer-sign
-I<fuzzy>/include best.c -o best.

@endnode

@node g-matching "Matching"
@next g-how-the-ranking-works
@prev g-the-first-program

@{b}Matching@{ub}

    FuzzyPattern *p = FuzzyPrepare("csrc", 0);              /* once */
    LONG s = FuzzyScore(p, "config/source.c", -1, NULL, 0); /* for each text: 0 = no match */
    FuzzyFreePattern(p);

  - @{b}Prepare once, score many times.@{ub} FuzzyPrepare folds the pattern and
    builds its tables; FuzzyScore is then cheap. A higher score is a
    better match; 0 is none. Sort by it.
  - @{b}The letters in order, with gaps:@{ub} csrc matches config/source.c, not
    config/sauce.c.
  - @{b}Several words@{ub} (a space between) must all match, in any order: mod
    music finds Work:Music/mod.foo. An empty pattern matches everything
    with score 1.
  - @{b}Case and accents do not matter@{ub} by default: goteborg finds Göteborg,
    WORK finds Work:.
  - @{b}Where the letters were@{ub}, for highlighting: pass a UWORD array and its
    size; it gets the byte position of each matched letter (in pattern
    order, word after word).

    UWORD pos[32];
    if (FuzzyScore(p, text, -1, pos, 32))
        ...  /* pos[0] is where the first letter of the pattern was found */

  FPF_CASE
    case matters
  FPF_ACCENTS
    accents matter (o no longer finds ö)
  FPF_PLAIN
    each word as one piece of text, no gaps (src no longer matches s/r/c)
  FPF_TEXT
    the texts are not paths (history, commands): no file-name bonus, and a
      word after a space counts more

Text is Latin-1, as Amiga file names are. Texts longer than 65535 bytes
are scored on their first 65535.

@endnode

@node g-how-the-ranking-works "How the ranking works"
@next g-the-index-reading-and-searching
@prev g-matching

@{b}How the ranking works@{ub}

It is fzf's ranking (its "v1" way and its numbers), with its @{b}path scheme@{ub}
by default:

  1. Find the pattern's letters in order, left to right; then from the
     last one backwards to the shortest window that holds them all.
  2. Score that window: 16 a letter; more for a letter at the @{b}start of a@{ub}
     @{b}word@{ub} (after / : _ - . space, a capital after a small letter, a digit
     after a letter), more again for letters @{b}in a row@{ub}; a small penalty for
     each gap. The first letter's bonus counts double.
  3. @{b}The file name wins:@{ub} if the letters fit in the name (after the last /
     or :) that window is tried too, and a window wholly in the name gets
     a bonus. So dpaint puts Graphics/DPaint/DPaint above
     Docs/dpaint-notes/a.txt.
  4. Ties (in FuzzySearch): the shorter path first, then the one that came
     first.

: counts as a path separator, as on the Amiga. On a 21,028-path test list
from a real A1200 the top five for dpaint, startup, ahiru, mod and readme
were the same as fzf 0.74's, in the same order (checked 2026-10-17).

@endnode

@node g-the-index-reading-and-searching "The index: reading and searching"
@next g-the-index-writing
@prev g-how-the-ranking-works

@{b}The index: reading and searching@{ub}

    struct FuzzyIndex *x = FuzzyOpenIndex(NULL, &err);   /* NULL: @{"FuzzyIndexPath()" link "FuzzyIndexPath"}'s place */

x->Count is how many entries it has, x->Created when it was made (seconds
since 1978-01-01, as AmigaDOS counts), x->Volumes how many volumes it
covers (FuzzyVolume(x, n, &date) gives each name).

@{b}Every entry, in order:@{ub}

    struct FuzzyEntry e;
    while (FuzzyNextEntry(x, &e))
        printf("%s %s %lu\\n", e.Type == FET_DRAWER ? "D" : "F", e.Path, e.Size);

  Path, PathLen
    "Work:Music/mod.foo", valid until the next FuzzyNextEntry
  NameOffset
    where the name starts in Path
  Type
    FET_FILE, FET_DRAWER, FET_LINK (a soft link, never followed)
  Size
    bytes (0 for a drawer)
  Date
    seconds since 1978-01-01
  Prot
    the protection bits (hsparwed)

FuzzyRewind(x) starts again from the first.

@{b}The best matches:@{ub} FuzzySearch(x, pattern, flags, max) returns the max
best, best first, in a struct FuzzyResults (Count hits in Hits[], and
Matched: how many matched in all). Each hit has the entry's fields and its
Score; the paths are copies, valid until FuzzyFreeResults. Flags: the FPF_
ones, and:

  @{b}Flag@{ub}         @{b}Means@{ub}
  -----------  ---------------------------------------------
  FSF_FILES    only files
  FSF_DRAWERS  only drawers
  FSF_NAME     match only the name, not the drawers above it

@{b}Where the index is:@{ub} FuzzyIndexPath(buf, size) gives the place: the ENV:
setting FuzzyFind/Index, else S:FuzzyFind.index (not ENVARC:, which is
copied to RAM at every boot).

@endnode

@node g-the-index-writing "The index: writing"
@next g-the-index-format
@prev g-the-index-reading-and-searching

@{b}The index: writing@{ub}

FuzzyFind's updatedb builds the index of the disks. A program can write
one of its own (a catalogue of a CD, of an archive, of the drawers it has
visited) the same way:

    FuzzyWriter *w = FuzzyNewIndex("RAM:my.index", &err);   /* NULL name: the usual place */
    FuzzyAddVolume(w, "Work", volumedate);                   /* all volumes first */
    ...
    e.Path = "Work:Music/mod.foo"; e.PathLen = -1; e.Type = FET_FILE; ...
    FuzzyAddEntry(w, &e);                                    /* each file and drawer */
    ...
    err = FuzzyEndIndex(w, TRUE);                            /* TRUE: keep it; FALSE: throw it away */

  - The index is written to NAME.new and renamed over NAME by
    FuzzyEndIndex(w, TRUE), so a program reading the old one never sees
    half a new one.
  - @{b}Add a drawer's entries, then walk into its drawers@{ub} (as updatedb does):
    neighbours then share most of their path, which is what keeps the
    index small.
  - Paths up to 1024 bytes; a longer one is refused (FuzzyAddEntry returns
    FALSE) and the rest goes on.

@endnode

@node g-the-index-format "The index format"
@next g-speed
@prev g-the-index-writing

@{b}The index format@{ub}

Format 1, all numbers big-endian; "v" is a variable-length number: 7 bits
a byte, low bits first, the top bit set on every byte but the last.

    "FFIX"   UWORD 1 (the format)   UWORD 0 (flags)
    ULONG created (seconds since 1978-01-01)   ULONG entries
    UWORD volumes, then for each: UBYTE length, the name, ULONG the volume's date
    then each entry:
      v shared   bytes the path shares with the path before
      v rest     then that many bytes: the path is the previous one's first "shared" bytes + these
      UBYTE type (FET_)   UBYTE protection   v size   v date

Paths are Latin-1, volume names not device names (Work: not DH1:). A
reader refuses a format it does not know (FERR_VERSION). A real A1200's
index: 57,028 entries in 1.04 MB (a 21,028-path test list: 340 KB, against
966 KB as a plain list).

@endnode

@node g-speed "Speed"
@next g-arexx
@prev g-the-index-format

@{b}Speed@{ub}

On a real A1200 with a PiStorm (Emu68, which runs as a fast 68040; the
files on an SD card, so not typical of an Amiga), measured 2026-10-17:
updatedb indexed 57,028 files and drawers in 5.3 s, reading the index took
0.14 s, and one search 0.14-0.30 s.

@{b}Not measured on a stock Amiga yet.@{ub} Measured in FS-UAE (2026-10-17) with
the index in RAM:, so the processor's share only, one search of a
21,028-path test list took:

  @{b}Machine (emulated)@{ub}                     @{b}One search@{ub}
  -------------------------------------  ----------
  68000, 7 MHz, chip memory only (A600)  14-28 s
  68020, 14 MHz, fast memory (A1200)     3-6 s

A search reads every entry, so it grows with the size of the index, and on
a real machine reading the index from its drive comes on top (that depends
on the drive: not measured). Two things help:

  - @{b}A letter fingerprint:@{ub} while the entries are read, the library notes
    which letters each path has (32 bits). A path missing one of the
    pattern's letters is skipped without being scored: goteborg took half
    the time of dpaint.
  - @{b}Narrowing:@{ub} a program that keeps the matches (as fzf does) only needs
    to look again at them when a letter is added; the first letter is the
    slow one.

@endnode

@node g-arexx "ARexx"
@next g-versions
@prev g-speed

@{b}ARexx@{ub}

    CALL ADDLIB('fuzzy.library', 0, -30, 0)
    SAY FUZZYSCORE('csrc', 'config/source.c')     /* a number: 0 = no match */
    SAY FUZZYLOCATE('dpaint', 5)                  /* the 5 best paths of the index, one a line */

  FUZZYSCORE(pattern, text, [options])
    the score: higher is better, 0 no match
  FUZZYLOCATE(pattern, [max], [options])
    the best max paths of the index (20 if not given), one a line ('0a'x
      between); '' if none, or if there is no index (FUZZYERROR() says)
  FUZZYERROR()
    why the last FUZZYLOCATE gave nothing

Options, any of, in one string: PLAIN CASE ACCENTS TEXT, and for
FUZZYLOCATE also FILES DRAWERS NAME. A line of results longer than an
ARexx string (65535 bytes) gives ''; ask for fewer.

@endnode

@node g-versions "Versions"
@next g-rules-and-traps
@prev g-arexx

@{b}Versions@{ub}

While fuzzy.library is young its version is @{b}0@{ub} and its @{b}revision@{ub} counts up
(0.1, 0.2...). Open version 0 and check the revision before using newer
calls:

  @{b}Revision@{ub}  @{b}Added@{ub}
  --------  ----------
  0.1       everything

Calls are never removed or moved. The index format has its own number (1),
checked by the reader.

@endnode

@node g-rules-and-traps "Rules and traps"
@prev g-versions

@{b}Rules and traps@{ub}

  - @{b}Free what you get:@{ub} FuzzyFreePattern, FuzzyCloseIndex,
    FuzzyFreeResults, FuzzyEndIndex (always, even to throw an index away).
  - FuzzyNextEntry@{b}'s@{ub} Path @{b}is overwritten by the next call:@{ub} copy it to keep
    it. FuzzySearch's hits are copies, safe until FuzzyFreeResults.
  - @{b}One index handle per task.@{ub} Several tasks may open the same index file,
    each with its own FuzzyOpenIndex.
  - @{b}The index is a snapshot:@{ub} files made after updatedb ran are not in it.
    Check a path still exists before acting on it.
  - FuzzySearch @{b}goes through the whole index@{ub} each time: in an interactive
    program keep the matches and narrow them as the user types (see
    FuzzyFind's fzf).

@endnode

